Combat AI Scripting:Cythera supports user created combat AI.  Combat AI is a series of rules (tests and actions).  During combat, this series of rules is evaluated to determine who to attack, etc...A rule is composed of two parts - the test, and then a list of weighted actions.  The test section is one or more condition, and if those conditions are true, then the action section is performed.   The actions are broken down into weighted sections, only one of which is performed (making it easy to sometimes do one thing, sometimes another).First, a simple example:;=======================================================================NAME "Sample AI"; run away if serious or criticalIF CurHealthLessThan(myself, Wounded) THEN	#100	Retreat()	; if we don't have a target, pick a new one - either close or healthiestIF !Exists(target) THEN	#50		SetTarget(enemy.ByRange.Pick)	#50		SetTarget(enemy.ByHealth.Pick);=======================================================================What this example does, is if our health is less than Wounded (serious or critical), we retreat.  Otherwise, if we don't have a current target, then 50% of the time we target the closest enemy, and 50% of the time we target the healthiest enemy.Individuals and Groups----------------------One of the most important part of scripting AI is the idea of individuals and groups.  An individual is just that - a since creature.  A group is a, well, a group of individuals, but more importantly, each individual has a ranking within the group.  Most conditions and actions are based on an individual (who to attack, for example) - it is selecting that individual that matters.  To do this, there are a number of "base" individuals and groups, and then a bunch of modifiers that take that individual/group and transform it into another.Base Individuals:	myself		The individual that these rules are being applied to	main		The main (player) character	target		The current target of myself	[name]		A specific character, by nameBase Groups:	good		All good individuals	evil		All evil individuals	netural		All neutral individuals	feral		All feral (wild) individuals	ally		All allies of myself	enemy		All enemies of myself	bystander	All individuals that aren't allies or enemies of myself	everybody	All individualsModifiers (individual->individual)	CurTarget	Who this individual is attacking	Protecting	Who this individual is marked as protecting	LastAttacker The last individual that did damage to this individual.Modifiers (individual->group)	Allies		Everybody on this individuals side	Enemies		Everybody against this individual	Attackers	Everybody currently attacking this individual	ProtectedBy	Everybody marked as protecting this individualModifiers (group->group)	ByRange		Re-rank according to distance from myself	ByDamage	Re-rank according to how badly hurt they are	ByHealth	Re-rank according to how healthy they are	ByStrongest	Re-rank according to how strong they are	ByWeakest	Re-rank according to how weak they are	Randomize	Throw some randomness into the rankingModifiers (group->individual)	Best		Take the highest ranked one	Random		Randomly pick one, based on ranking	Pick		Halfway between "Best" and "Random"Test----These form the heart of a condition - a test evaluates something, and returns true or false.  Many tests have parameters, and usually the first parameter is an individual.	True()			Always returns "true"	False()			Always returns "false"	IsSpecies(individual, species)			Returns true is an individual is a given species.  Valid values for species are:	IsA(individual, basegroup)			Returns true if an individual in in a given base group (good, evil, ally, enemy, etc...)	IsTarget(individual)			Returns true if myself's current target the same as this individual.	HealthIs(individual, health)			Returns true if the individual's health exactly is a given value.  Valid values for health are:		Dead		Critical		Serious		Wounded		Scratch		Complete	HealthAtLeast(individual, health)			Returns true if the individual's health is at least as good as "health".  See "HealthIs()" for valid values	HealthLessThan(individual, health)			Returns true if the individual's health is worse than "health".  See "HealthIs()" for valid values	InParty(individual)			Returns true if the individual is in the players party	BeenMet(individual)			Returns true if the individual has been met	InRange(individual, range)			Returns true if the individual is within a given range, measured in "movement squares"	Random(number)			Returns true if a random number from 1 to 100 is less than or equal to the given number (Random(100) is always true, Random(0) is never true)	TestFlag(individual, flag)			Returns true if the specific flag for the individual is set.  Value values of "flag" are:			IsAlive			IsPoisoned			IsEnhorsed			IsAngry			IsRegen			IsFear			IsParalyse			IsInvisible			IsXray			IsCharmed			IsNightVision			IsCursed			IsBlessed			IsConfused			IsSleep			IsLavaProof	Exists(individual)			Returns true if the individual exists, and is "on stage".		HasSpell(spellnumber)			Returns true if myself has the given spell	ManaAtLeast(number)			Returns true if myself has at least this many mana points	HasMeleeWeapon(individual)			Returns true if the individual has a melee weapon	HasRangedWeapon(individual, range)			Returns true if the individual has a ranged weapon able to reach the given range (and has the ammo to go with it, if needed)	UsingMeleeWeapon(individual)			Returns true if the individual is actually using a melee weapon	UsingRangedWeapon(individual,range)			Returns true if the individual is actually using a ranged weapon	InLOS(individual)			Return true if individual is currently in line of sight from myselfConditions----------A condition is a test combined with some "flow" control.  These take the following four forms:	IF test THEN	IF NOT test THEN	IF test OR	IF NOT test ORIn the first two forms, if the test is evaluated as true or false (respectively), then the next condition or action is performed.  If the test fails, then it skips down to the next rule.  In the second two forms, if the test evaluates correctly, it automatically skips to the actions for this rule.  If not, it goes to the next condition (you should never have an "OR" before your action section of a rule).For example:	IF True() THEN		[ is executed - all conditions met ]		IF True() THEN	IF False() THEN		[ isn't executed - second condition fails ]		IF True() OR	IF False() THEN				[ is executed - first condition is met ]		IF True() THEN	IF True() OR	IF False() THEN		[ is executed - first and second conditions are met ]	IF False() OR	IF True() THEN		[ is still executed - first condition fails, but second is met ]	IF False() OR	IF True() THEN	IF False() THEN		[ is not exectued - first and third conditions fail ]Actions-------Actions are a list of things to do if a condition is met.  The combat AI system supports having more than one action, or set of actions, performed upon meeting a condition.  Each list of actions begins with a weight, expressed as a percent.  You should have the total of all weights for a list of actions in a rule equal to 100 - if it is less than that, it may not execute any of the action.  By using this weighting system, it is easy to set up actions that are normally done, with random things that are occasionally done.Each action is similar to a test - it is the name of an action, followed by optional parameters.  Like a test, the first parameter is usually an individual (but not always).  This parameter would take the same form of base + modifiers that it would in a test.	Continue()			Causes execution to skip ahead to next rule, instead of terminating at the end of this list of actions as normal.	SetTarget(individual)			Set the target of myself to a new individual	Retreat()			Exit from combat and run away (no further evaluation of rules is done until the next combat).	Pass(ticks)			Do nothing for however long	CastSpell(target, spellnum)			Cast a spell on a given target (not all spells take targets)	WalkTo(other)			Walk until next to the "other"	BattleCry()			Causes the character to shout out some sort of battle cry (doesn't actually do anything useful beyond that though)	EquipMelee()			Try to equip a melee weapon, if possible	EquipRanged(range)			Try to equip a ranged weapon, if possible, with a range at least as large as the given parameter	RunAwayFrom(individual, distance)			Retreat back so that we are a given distance away from that individual	RunToward(individual, distance)			Go toward an individual, until we are are at not more than a given distance away from them	DoAttack()			Attack the currently selected target, if possible (if too far away, this will close in for the attack).	FinishCombat()			Stop attacking and return to whatever is normally done	SetProtecting(individual)			Set the "protecting" field of the current individual to point to the new individual (this can be accessed then via the Protecting and ProtectedBy modifiers)Combat AI Files---------------A combat AI file is a text file that start with the name of the behavior, followed by the list of rules.  Lines that begin with a semicolon (';') are comments.  See the samples below for more examples.;=======================================================================NAME "Sample AI 2"; run away if serious or criticalIF CurHealthLessThan(myself, Wounded) THEN	#100	Retreat(); if we don't have a target, pick a new one - either close or healthiestIF !Exists(target) THEN	#50		SetTarget(enemy.ByRange.Pick)			DoAttack()	#50		SetTarget(enemy.ByHealth.Pick)			DoAttack()	; Are we attacking the nastiest creature?  If so, try to pick a new one; but only if there isn't another one availableIF IsTarget(enemy.ByStrongest.Best) THENIF IsTarget(enemy.ByWeakest.Best) THEN; never mind - only one, keep at it...	#100	DoAttack()IF IsTarget(enemy.ByStrongest.Best) THEN	#100	SetTarget(enemy.ByHealth.Pick)			DoAttack()IF Exists(target) THEN	#100	DoAttack();=======================================================================